<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dendera Temple complex</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Temple of Hathor at Dendera</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dendera Temple complex lies on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qena">Qena</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>. Its main temple, dedicated to the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, was begun in the last decades of the Ptolemaic period, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII</a>, and completed under Roman emperors. It is one of the best-preserved temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, famous for its painted ceilings, a relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleopatra-vii">Cleopatra VII</a> and the Dendera zodiac.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dendera-temple-complex.jpg" alt="Dendera Temple complex" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ijanderson977 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dendera_7_977.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
